PAGE RISKS (builder handoff, judged from the page specs)

- Three tail-tip pages (horse, tap-tap-tap, tap-tap-tap-b): all three colorPrompts carry a strong guard. The horse page says ONLY the fluffy golden-brown tip of a tail sticks up, lists no puppy body, no head, no ears, no legs, and ends with exactly one girl, one horse, and one tail tip. tap-tap-tap says ONLY the tip shows behind the big basket and ends with exactly two boys, two mats, and one tail tip. tap-tap-tap-b says ONLY the tip lies on the floor, bans the rest of the puppy and motion lines, and ends with exactly two boys and one tail tip. The prompts are clear. Each spec holds a Pip reference: out/storytime/l2/unit1/w04/muse/elements/pip-ref-render.png, so the model sees the real tail color and fluff. Advice: at page review, check one closed golden-brown fluffy tip and no other puppy part; if a whole puppy returns, re-run with the ONLY sentence first in the picture block.
- Map page (pat-can-see-a-map): the colorPrompt is explicit: behind the lifted corner the wall is plain empty cream plaster, no hole, no door, no picture, and nothing hidden. The goal repeats: he looks at the empty wall behind it. The set wall carries dark timber beams, and the map hangs low, so the lifted lower-right corner sits close to a beam line. The prompt is clear. This spec holds no Pip reference, and none is needed, because nothing hides behind the corner; its references are Pat and the book-corner set. Advice: at page review, check that nothing shows behind the lifted corner and that the exposed wall reads as plain wall; a beam crossing that area is part of the set wall, not a hidden object; if the model draws a hidden picture or door, re-run with the ban sentence kept.
- Narrow floor strip behind the basket (tap-tap-tap, tap-tap-tap-b, he-can-see-pip): the m2 set keeps the basket close to the wall. The two tap pages need only a small tail tip in the strip, which fits. he-can-see-pip needs a whole puppy in that strip, which is tight; its colorPrompt says Pip lies in the open strip of floor between the basket and the wall with his whole body in view. The wording is clear; the space is the risk. he-can-see-pip holds a Pip reference, so the model can copy the real puppy. Advice: at page review, check that the tip shows in the strip on both tap pages and that Pip fits whole on he-can-see-pip; if Pip cannot fit, ask for a set m3 with the basket moved away from the wall, or re-run with the sentence that Pip lies in front of and beside the basket.

